Use the full-stack parent instructions first, then the frontend child instructions whenever the task involves frontend code, styling, route composition, component design, frontend docs, or backend integration from the UI.

For frontend feature requests, the parent instructions include a clarification-first workflow:

- ask 3 important questions when the request is ambiguous
- confirm route, interaction flow, and UI states before implementation
- confirm or recommend API inputs and outputs when data is involved
- reuse an existing section or component pattern when relevant
- keep the visual language consistent
- design pages like real product UI, not filler-heavy showcase screens
- treat the repo as a SaaS workspace by default rather than a marketing template
- keep copy brief and useful
- visually integrate third-party controls into the surrounding theme
- design flows before screens and verify state handling, recovery, and mobile fit
- keep domain rules out of presentation components and keep UI responsibilities local to the surface
- avoid decorative filler, nested cards, and generic hero layouts when building actual product pages
- keep auth and access pages especially lean: utility-first layout, one primary action, no promotional card grids unless explicitly requested
- default to safer frontend persistence: do not drop secrets into browser storage and be explicit about cache freshness for each new data surface
- treat browser caching as a product decision with scenarios: `no-store` for auth and user-sensitive data, short-lived or focus-based revalidation for dashboards and search, and longer caching only for public reference content
- commit to a clear aesthetic direction and avoid generic AI-generated frontend patterns
- self-review hierarchy, spacing, states, and mobile fit before finishing
- verify with lint, typecheck, and build

For frontend documentation work, prefer Mermaid-first architecture docs with concise supporting text.

